.TH "nydus-server" "1" "May 2025" "Christopher Irving" ""
.SH "NAME"
.LP 
nydus\-server \- Nydus Launcher server program which authenticates Minecraft accounts and allocates them to clients. 
.SH "SYNTAX"
.LP 
nydus\-server
.LP
nydus\-server --version
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.LP 
Upon startup,
.BR nydus-server
attempts to authenticate all the Minecraft accounts it has been given.
This usually involves opening a browser and requires a human to log each account in.
.BR nydus-server
will then store all account authentication tokens in a local file
and listen for connection attempts from
.BR nydus-client (1)
instances on the network.
Clients may either request a Minecraft account be allocated to them, renew an allocation
already made to them, or release an account already allocated to them.
.BR nydus-server
keeps track of all authenticated Minecraft accounts that can be used, and the
IP address, system username, time of allocation, and time of renewal for each
account in use by a client. If an allocation is not renewed for too long, the
server will release that allocation, as a precaution against failures on the client.

.BR nydus-server
will periodically renew authentication tokens which are close to expiring, so there
should be no need for a human to manually authenticate any accounts again after
the server is first started.

The server stores authentication tokens for all accounts and information on which
accounts are allocated (collectively called the "allocation database") in a file
on the system. The location of the allocation database is configured through
.IR /etc/nydus/nydus-server.conf,
but it defaults to
.IR /etc/nydus/nydus-alloc.csv.
The server will overwrite this file each time it starts up. There is no mechanic
to prepare an allocation database and then load it into the server upon startup.
You need to manually reauthenticate all the accounts you want to use through
.BR nydus-server
each time you restart it.

The allocation database is re-written to the file each time an allocation or
authentication change happens to one of the Minecraft accounts.

The only command line argument
.BR nydus-server
accepts is
.BR --version.
If given this argument,
.BR nydus-server
will simply print the version of the currently installed Nydus Launcher software
and exit, instead of performing its normal task of maintaining Minecraft
account allocations.

.BR nydus-server
is entirely configured by options in the configuration
file found at
.IR /etc/nydus/nydus-server.conf.
See
.BR nydus-server.conf (5)
for details on the configuration file's format.

You can start the Nydus Launcher server by simply invoking
.BR nydus-server
from the command line, but it is recommended to instead
use the systemd service included in the Debian package.

While running,
.BR nydus-server
outputs log messages to
.IR /var/log/nydus/nydus-server.log.
It empties this file each time it is restarted.

The Nydus Launcher suite also includes the program
.BR nydus-cli (1)
which is intended to manually manipulate the database of
Minecraft account allocations, such as manually allocating
or releasing a particular account, while the Nydus Launcher server
is running. This should not ordinarily be needed, but may
be useful if the server misperforms in unexpected circumstances.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.LP
.BR nydus-server.conf (5),
.BR nydus-client (1),
.BR nydus-cli (1)
